# 987. Vertical Order Traversal of a Binary Tree

## APPROACH: VERTICAL ORDER TRAVERSAL (COORDINATE SYSTEM + MULTI-LEVEL SORTING)

Idea:
Har node ko 2D plane par ek coordinate maano:
- Root starts at `(row = 0, col = 0)`.
- Left child jaata hai `(row + 1, col - 1)`.
- Right child jaata hai `(row + 1, col + 1)`.

Question ka sorting order constraint bohot specific hai:
1. Column-wise: Leftmost column se rightmost column (`col` increasing).
2. Row-wise: Agar column same hai, toh upar se neeche (`row` increasing).
3. Value-wise: Agar `col` aur `row` dono same hon (overlap), toh values naturally sorted honi chahiye (`val` increasing).

--------------------------------------------------

APPROACH (DFS / Preorder Traversal + Custom Sorting)

1. Coordinate Collection:
   - Ek helper class `Point` banao: `(val, row, col)`.
   - Tree ko traverse karke (DFS / BFS) saare nodes ke coordinates ek list `points` me store karo:
     - Left call: `traverse(root.left, row + 1, col - 1, points)`
     - Right call: `traverse(root.right, row + 1, col + 1, points)`

2. 3-Tier Comparator Sorting:
   `points` list ko 3 conditions ke basis par sort karo:
   - If `a.col != b.col`: `a.col - b.col` (Left to Right columns)
   - Else if `a.row != b.row`: `a.row - b.row` (Top to Bottom rows)
   - Else: `a.val - b.val` (Overlapping cells me smaller value pehle)

3. Grouping by Column:
   - Sorted points ko traverse karo aur `LinkedHashMap<Integer, List<Integer>>` me `col` ke basis par group karo.
   - Map ki saari values ko list of lists bana kar return kar do: `new ArrayList<>(map.values())`.

--------------------------------------------------

EXAMPLE

Tree:
          1 (0,0)
        /   \
    2 (1,-1) 3 (1,1)
    /   \    /   \
4(2,-2) 5(2,0) 6(2,0) 7(2,2)

Dry Run:
Points Collected:
(1, 0, 0), (2, 1, -1), (4, 2, -2), (5, 2, 0), (3, 1, 1), (6, 2, 0), (7, 2, 2)

After 3-Tier Sorting:
- col -2: (4, 2, -2)
- col -1: (2, 1, -1)
- col  0:
  - row 0: (1, 0, 0)
  - row 2: (5, 2, 0) aur (6, 2, 0) -> dono same spot par hain, val sort hogi -> 5, 6
  => [1, 5, 6]
- col  1: (3, 1, 1)
- col  2: (7, 2, 2)

Result: [[4], [2], [1, 5, 6], [3], [7]]

--------------------------------------------------

WHY THIS SORTING LOGIC?

- Normal BFS/Queue se Level Order karte waqt same level ke nodes automatically `row` sorted toh hote hain, par jab do nodes same `(row, col)` par collide karti hain (e.g., node 5 aur 6), tab unhe numerically sort karna standard BFS queue me tricky ho jata hai.
- Sare points ko collect karke ek hi custom comparator se sort karna implementation ko extremely clean aur bug-free bana deta hai.

--------------------------------------------------

COMPLEXITY

N = Total number of nodes in tree

Time:
O(N log N) ->
- Traversal: O(N) har node visit hoti hai.
- Sorting: N points ko sort karne me O(N log N) time lagta hai.
- Map grouping: O(N).
Total Time = O(N log N).

Space:
O(N) ->
- `points` list: N elements.
- Recursive stack: O(H).
- Map & Result: O(N).
Total Space = O(N).

--------------------------------------------------

INTERVIEW POINT

- Trap Condition (The "Hard" part):
  Normal vertical order traversal me overlap par sorting nahi hoti (LeetCode standard vs Hard variation).
  Is question me:
  "There may be multiple nodes in the same row and same column. In such a case, sort these nodes by their values."
  Isi ek line ki wajah se comparator me 3rd condition (`a.val - b.val`) aati hai.
- BFS vs DFS:
  Dono se solve ho sakta hai. BFS me `Queue` + `TreeMap<Integer, TreeMap<Integer, PriorityQueue<Integer>>>` use hota hai, jo implement karne me thoda verbose hota hai. DFS + Comparator approach code readable aur concise rakhta hai.

